<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kleve</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cleves</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kleve, known in English as Cleves, is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border. Its Schwanenburg castle was the seat of the counts and, from 1417, dukes of Cleves, whose house also came to hold Mark, Ravenstein and Nevers. In 1521 the duchy was joined with Jülich and Berg, and after the disputed succession of 1609 Cleves passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kleve.jpg" alt="Kleve" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pieter Delicaat · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleve_Schwanenturm_vom_Kleiner_Markt_PM17-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick William, the Great Elector (r. 1640–1688), won full sovereignty over it from Poland in the Treaty of Wehlau in 1657 and built a standing army and a central tax administration across scattered domains that stretched from <b>Cleves</b> on the Rhine to Pomerania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He then built a central administration under the General War Commissariat that collected taxes across his scattered lands, from <b>Cleves</b> and Mark in the west to the Duchy of Prussia in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1368 the counts also inherited <b>Cleves</b>, and from 1398 Cleves and Mark were ruled together.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-mark">County of Mark</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1491 it passed to the house of <b>Cleves</b>, and in 1539 Francis I raised it to a duchy-peerage for François de Clèves.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nevers">County of Nevers</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1346 the county passed by marriage to the counts of Berg and later formed part of the united territory of Jülich, <b>Cleves</b> and Berg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">County of Ravensberg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1423 it was united with the Duchy of Jülich and in 1521 with <b>Cleves</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a></li></ul></section>
